WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
Weak passwords and poor recovery flows make credential attacks easier.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.900
Password policy, breached-password screening, recovery verification,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.200
and secure reset processes all contribute to the strength of authentication.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.860
Multifactor authentication reduces account takeover risk,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.000
but only when it is enforced consistently and resistant to bypass.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.580
Enrollment, recovery, administrative exceptions,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.360
and remembered-device logic need careful review.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:31.980
Session management determines whether authenticated access remains trustworthy after

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.000
login.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.980
Session tokens should be unpredictable, protected in transit and storage,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.480
rotated at appropriate times, and invalidated on logout or major account changes.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.180
Authentication controls must account for automated attack patterns such as credential

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.280
stuffing,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.420
password spraying, brute force attempts, and token replay.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:56.400
Require MFA for sensitive access and high-risk workflows.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.980
Use strong password and authenticator management practices.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.920
Protect sessions with secure token handling, expiration, and reauthentication.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:11.140
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include IA-2 - Identification and Authentication;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.040
IA-5 - Authenticator Management; AC-7 - Unsuccessful Logon Attempts.
